WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.789 --> 00:00:01.789
Hello, everyone!

00:00:01.789 --> 00:00:08.881
I will kick off my YouTube Series about explained
AI methods and papers with the question: How

00:00:08.881 --> 00:00:13.290
do we check if a neural network has learned
to solve A?

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:19.700
Well, the answer to this question is not so
interesting: Since we have trained the neural

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:23.930
network for solving A, we have training data
for A.

00:00:23.930 --> 00:00:31.240
Then, we test how well the network solves
A on the held-out set of data and we are done.

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:32.770
No, come on!

00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:37.890
Let us ask something more interesting: How
do we check if a neural network trained on

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:43.620
task A has learned a phenomenon specific to
task B?

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:50.070
And we will give the answer focusing on NLP,
but it is generalizable to many other applications,

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:52.150
like Computer Vision.

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:57.660
Since the AI field is changing so rapidly,
this video is a snapshot of the current state

00:00:57.660 --> 00:00:59.070
of research.

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:04.629
Simultaneously, it is my attempt to explain
intriguing but complicated concepts, that

00:01:04.629 --> 00:01:09.830
researchers use every day, in a simple and
understandable manner.

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:15.110
So let’s get back to the original question:
How do we check if a neural network trained

00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:20.100
on task A has learned a phenomenon specific
to task B?

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:25.960
We start here with a neural network and supposing
it was trained on large amounts of training

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:32.090
data like on Wikipedia, where it “reads”
the text and has to predict masked words.

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:34.680
[strange voice] Can a neural network read?

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:41.500
This masked word prediction is our task A.
Because predicting masked words by a neural

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:48.610
network has been lately proven to be extremely
effective on many other tasks apparently unrelated

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:53.049
to masked word prediction, we want to test
this:

00:01:53.049 --> 00:01:59.480
Is this true that if the neural network, trained
to complete missing parts of text, has also

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:02.729
captured interesting linguistic phenomena?

00:02:02.729 --> 00:02:08.450
And before we can answer this question, we
have to choose one phenomenon we want to probe

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:09.629
for.

00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:16.290
Checking if and how much a neural network
trained on task A, captures a specific (linguistic)

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:22.440
phenomenon, is referred to by terms like:
probing or diagnostic classifier or a "probing

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:24.079
task B".

00:02:24.079 --> 00:02:29.319
So the task B comprises the linguistic phenomenon
we want to test for here.

00:02:29.319 --> 00:02:34.849
Keeping it simple now, I will use the example
of sentiment expressed in text, where the

00:02:34.849 --> 00:02:38.060
text is no longer than a paragraph.

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:43.549
And here, a neural network has to tell us,
if a paragraph expresses positive sentiment,

00:02:43.549 --> 00:02:46.569
or negative sentiment.

00:02:46.569 --> 00:02:51.930
The example could be anything else, from the
grammaticality of a sentence to natural language

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:52.930
inference.

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:53.930
Now, back again to our So in this sentiment
analysis example, the task is simple:

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:58.249
Now back again to our original question: How
do we check if a neural network trained on

00:02:58.249 --> 00:03:02.329
task A has learned a phenomenon specific to
task B (in NLP)?

00:03:02.329 --> 00:03:06.309
We wonder: How to researchers try to answer
this question?

00:03:06.309 --> 00:03:08.260
Well, with probing.

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:10.889
Okay, okay, and how do we probe?

00:03:10.889 --> 00:03:17.590
We take the network as-is, trained on task
A, and remove the last part, the one that

00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:22.519
was responsible for mapping all the weights
encoding patterns in the data for making the

00:03:22.519 --> 00:03:24.340
final prediction.

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:30.180
Now to test if this part can also do sentiment
classification, we then replace to the last

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:36.519
layers with another fresh classifier that
has not yet been trained to do anything.

00:03:36.519 --> 00:03:42.349
It cannot now map the original parameters
of the neural network to sentiment classification.

00:03:42.349 --> 00:03:45.269
Okay, then let’s train it to!

00:03:45.269 --> 00:03:52.109
In order to keep everything preserved that
we have trained from task A, we freeze (so

00:03:52.109 --> 00:03:58.680
leave unchanged) the old part of the network,
and this fresh part, we train.

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:04.799
And train it on a relatively small dataset
of sentiment classification or task B, containing

00:04:04.799 --> 00:04:09.049
paragraphs labelled with positive or negative
sentiment.

00:04:09.049 --> 00:04:15.129
And in this scenario, the new classifier,
our probe, is performing binary classification,

00:04:15.129 --> 00:04:21.910
where the output is either 0 for negative
sentiment or 1 for positive sentiment.

00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:27.440
From the way the probe trains, we differentiate
between the following scenarios:

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:33.160
If the classifier, trained on the small dataset
for probing, is learning fast and performing

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:38.840
well, then the weights we left unchanged have
been already capturing the information of

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:40.580
the probe task.

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:46.530
In the second, worse scenario, if the classifier
trained on this small dataset is not learning

00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:51.330
anything and is barely performing better than
when it is untrained (or at the beginning

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:55.670
of training), then the weights of the big
neural net have been not capturing any information

00:04:55.670 --> 00:04:57.840
about the probing task at all.

00:04:57.840 --> 00:04:59.330
Or so it seems.

00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:04.410
Ok now, if we would ignore the most recent
research, we would stop here.

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:09.550
And if you clicked on this video for getting
the answer to the original question of “How

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:14.460
do we check if a neural network has learned
something (in NLP)?”, then you are ready

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:19.440
to go, since we explained how it works on
the example of sentiment analysis how this

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:20.440
is done.

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:24.940
And the same procedure holds for many other
phenomena and is not even restricted to NLP!

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:30.020
Now in the following, I will be addressing
the contribution of a recent paper, where

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:35.160
the authors address the following problems:
Neural Networks in general, tend to overfit,

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:40.400
meaning that they specialize on a certain
task, “memorizing” input and output.

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:46.760
And this problem gets even bigger when the
size of out training set is small, and this

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:49.860
is usually the case for our probing task.

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:55.020
Overfitting happens with the classifier too,
where it is able to learn the new probing

00:05:55.020 --> 00:06:01.410
task, but is also able to learn a completely
random probing task, where the labels have

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:02.890
been randomly altered.

00:06:02.890 --> 00:06:09.470
Also, sometimes, the expected difference between
an untrained probe and a trained one, is not

00:06:09.470 --> 00:06:11.490
as big as expected.

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:16.620
Can the authors do something to “save”
the standard probing from its problems?

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:18.980
Oh yes, they do!

00:06:18.980 --> 00:06:25.020
They use an information-theoretic view, and
since this might sound intimidating, the idea

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:30.640
is elegant, and yet so simple: Additionally
to measuring how many times the neural network

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:35.760
with the trained probe can give right or wrong
answers, they check if the neural network

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:39.510
can also efficiently transmit the data.

00:06:39.510 --> 00:06:46.300
This means: if we call our neural network
Bob and the probing classifier, Alice, the

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:53.330
person receiving the answers of Bob and mapping
them to labels, then: Bob sees some data,

00:06:53.330 --> 00:06:58.110
processes it through the neural network and
sends it to Alice.

00:06:58.110 --> 00:07:03.080
So Alice receives a message, tries to decode
and reconstruct the right label, but gives

00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:08.720
him more credit if the message from which
she could decode the label has been short.

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:15.300
Instead of measuring only the capacity of
Bob and Alice to get to the right answer,

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:21.790
we are now additionally measuring how well
Bob could send the message in a short, compressed

00:07:21.790 --> 00:07:27.700
form, for which we would assume, he had do
“understand more” and see patterns in

00:07:27.700 --> 00:07:29.400
this world.

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:34.950
If you liked this information-theoretic approach
and want to know more, read the paper!

00:07:34.950 --> 00:07:38.780
It is so well written, that it is really a
pleasure to read.

00:07:38.780 --> 00:07:41.840
I have linked it in the description of the
video, below.

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:47.669
Thanks for watching, do not forget to like
and subscribe!

00:07:47.669 --> 00:07:56.980
See you, next time…!

